<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of San Marino</h1><p class="st-meta">301 CE – present (approx.) · capital San Marino</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny republic on Monte Titano that claims to be the oldest surviving sovereign state and republic in the world, traditionally founded in 301.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-marinus">Saint Marinus</a>, a Christian stonemason from the island of Rab in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, founded a monastic community on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-titano">Monte Titano</a> on 3 September 301 to escape persecution under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>. A self-governing community around the monastery and fortress is documented from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, with an assembly of household heads, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arengo-san-marino">Arengo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>San Marino has been governed since at least the 13th century by two Captains Regent elected every six months, with a Great and General Council as legislature. Its statutes of 1600 served as a written constitution. It kept its independence through its mountain position, alliances with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-montefeltro">Montefeltro</a> and recognition by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> in 1631. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and Italian are dominant; the economy today rests on tourism, banking and stamps.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>San Marino survived the occupation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-alberoni">Cardinal Alberoni</a> in 1739–40, declined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> offer of more territory in 1797, sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> in 1849 and was left independent at Italian unification. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> in 1988 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1992.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-san-marino.jpg" alt="Republic of San Marino" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_San_Marino.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>301 CE – present (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San Marino</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>San Marino</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>San Marino, Most Serene Republic of San Marino</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1164&amp;focus=republic-of-san-marino" aria-label="Republic of San Marino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4979 622 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of San Marino lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5299 878L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5299 878h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5299 878h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks San Marino. <a href="/atlas?year=1164&amp;focus=republic-of-san-marino">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

